    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "An animal (possibly at a development stage) is a natural enemy of another animal (possibly at a development stage)."
    },
    "id" : "21"
  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsParasitoidOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "Ageniaspis citricola is a wasp parasite of the citrus leafminer larva, Phyllocnistis citrella.",
        "type" : "label"
      }, {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "Trichogramma wasps are released in crops to control the corn borer.",
        "type" : "label"
      } ]
    },
    "label" : {
      "IRI-based" : "IsParasitoidOf",
      "undefined" : "is parasitoid of"
    },
    "subClasses" : [ "7" ],
    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "An animal (possibly at a development stage) is a parasitoid of an animal (possibly at a development stage) that it inevitably kills during or at the end of its development. An insect is said to be host of its parasitoid. Likewise, a parasitoid is host of its hyperparasitoid. Parasitoidism is a special case of parasitism in conjunction with predation (but different from PreysOn, which implies a brief relation). Lethality is the norm (majority of cases). If the host does not die, this usually means the death of the parasitoid."
    },
    "id" : "8",
    "superClasses" : [ "21" ]
  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsVectorOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "Xylella fastidiosa is a bacterium that spreads from one plant to another through sap-sucking insects, such as leafhoppers, which feed on plant sap and transmit the bacterium as they move between plants.",
        "type" : "label"
      } ]
    },
    "label" : {
      "IRI-based" : "IsVectorOf",
      "undefined" : "is vector of"
    },
    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "An animal is a vector of an animal/microbe (transmitted) for a host plant. The transmitted organism may be pathogenic (but not necessarily) for the host plant. Vector is understood here in the biotic sense, not to be confused with an abiotic vector, which is a means of dissemination (of a pest or pathogen). Vector is a type of commensalism: the transmitted animal is commensal of the vector animal."
    },
    "id" : "25",
    "superClasses" : [ "26" ]
  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsBeneficialPlantOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "The flowers of yarrow (Achillea millefolium) provide nectar and pollen to various auxiliary insects, such as bees or hoverflies and some parasitoids. Hoverfly larvae can then consume aphids present on the crops. Bees allow the pollination of the beneficiary crop.",
        "type" : "label"
      } ]
    },
    "label" : {
      "IRI-based" : "IsBeneficialPlantOf",
      "undefined" : "is beneficial plant of"
    },
    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "Cultivated or non-cultivated plant, intentionally introduced or encouraged or maintained, providing an agronomic or ecological benefit for a crop (called beneficiary). Examples: protecting crops from a bioaggressor, either by directly attracting the bioaggressor (trap plant) to draw it away from the crops, or by attracting auxiliary insects (predators, parasitoids, food insects of vectors, etc.)."
    },
    "id" : "10",
    "superClasses" : [ "27" ]
  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsPrimaryHostPlantOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "For Candidatus Phytoplasma vitis, the flavescence dorée phytoplasma, the primary host is the cultivated grapevine (Vitis vinifera).",
        "type" : "label"
      } ]
    },
    "label" : {
      "IRI-based" : "IsPrimaryHostPlantOf",
      "undefined" : "is primary host plant of"
    },
    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "Main cultivated plant on which a pest, a pathogen or an organism develops, reproduces or causes damage in an optimal way. Often the target crop or the most susceptible plant."
    },
    "id" : "28",
    "superClasses" : [ "27" ]
  }, {
