  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsParasiteOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "Trichogramma brassicae, a small parasitoid wasp, which parasitizes the eggs of certain pest insects, notably the corn borer (Ostrinia nubilalis).",
        "type" : "label"
      } ]
    },
    "label" : {
      "IRI-based" : "IsParasiteOf",
      "undefined" : "is parasite of"
    },
    "subClasses" : [ "13" ],
    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "An animal (possibly at a development stage) is a parasite of an animal (possibly at a development stage). Lethality is not necessary for the parasite (unlike parasitoidism), even though the host may die in some cases."
    },
    "id" : "23",
    "superClasses" : [ "21" ]
  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsRefugeOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "During winter, the hazel (Corylus avellana) is a refuge for predatory mites (Phytoseiidae) of phytophagous mites such as the red mite (Panonychus ulmi); in spring, the predatory mites can colonize the grapevine (Vitis vinifera) and take part in regulation.",
        "type" : "label"
      }, {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "The psyllid Cacopsylla pruni, vector of the phytoplasma 'Candidatus Phytoplasma prunorum', leaves Prunus in summer and uses conifers such as Norway spruce (Picea abies) as overwintering hosts; in early spring, adults return to fruit trees of the genus Prunus, such as plum (Prunus domestica), where they reproduce and can transmit the phytoplasma.",
        "type" : "label"
      } ]
    },
    "label" : {
      "IRI-based" : "IsRefugeOf",
      "undefined" : "is refuge of"
    },
    "comment" : {
      "undefined" : "A refuge plant is a non-cultivated plant, offering habitat, food (sap, alternative prey) or protection (egg-laying site) to a harmful or beneficial insect, during unfavorable conditions (cold, drought, temporary absence of the crop). The insect can be a pest, a vector (itself host of the pest or pathogen of something else), or a parasitoid of a pest."
    },
    "id" : "39",
    "superClasses" : [ "27" ]
  }, {
    "iri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology/IsCommensalOf",
    "baseIri" : "http://ns.inria.fr/ecocontrol/ontology",
    "instances" : 0,
    "annotations" : {
      "example" : [ {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "Some epiphytic bacteria, such as Pseudomonas spp., use the exudates present on the surface of the leaves of a cultivated plant as a source of nutrients and as a habitat, without significantly affecting the plant.",
        "type" : "label"
      }, {
        "identifier" : "example",
        "language" : "undefined",
        "value" : "Vector insects are possibly hosts of commensal microbes (rare), actually more often mutualists or parasites, rather than strict commensals.",
